# Backup settings for this server.
#
# Copy to /etc/cashmere/backup.env on the server (root-only: chmod 600) and fill
# in. The nightly backup reads it; so does the backup taken before each deploy.
# See deploy/RECOVERY.md for how each value is obtained.

# The age public key the offsite copies are sealed to (starts with "age1").
# Generate the key pair somewhere that is NOT this server:
#   age-keygen -o cashmere-backup.key      # prints the public key
# Keep cashmere-backup.key in the owner's password manager, and a second copy
# offline. Without it no offsite copy can ever be opened.
CASHMERE_BACKUP_RECIPIENT=

# Where the sealed copies go: an rclone remote configured on this server with
# `rclone config`, on storage this server does not administer. Give it keys
# that can write but not delete, and let the storage keep old versions.
#   b2:cashmere-backups/os
CASHMERE_OFFSITE=

# A dead-man's switch: a check at healthchecks.io (or anything that speaks the
# same /start and /fail URLs), expecting one ping a day with a few hours' grace.
# It alerts when a run fails and — the part a script cannot do for itself —
# when no run happens at all.
CASHMERE_BACKUP_PING_URL=

# Fail the run, and so alert, once the disk is this full.
CASHMERE_DISK_ALERT_PERCENT=85
